Title: "Current Implementation Notes"
Id: "d29002a5-f9a7-4223-8033-32a2a718ce45"

Text: "This page summarizes the behavior implemented by the current Rust code in the
main `mathlingua` repository. It is intentionally rule-focused and should be
read together with the detailed syntax pages in this reference."
Id: "ce52d87b-92fd-4974-856d-57ff257440bd"

SectionTitle: "Structural Language"
Id: "74303346-1df0-4524-9ae3-2c2e5e55f73c"

SubsectionTitle: "Markdown MathLingua Fences"
Id: "f93b33c9-60f4-45c4-9947-d966841c25c6"

Text: "`Text:` Markdown may contain fenced blocks tagged `mlg`. These blocks render as
MathLingua source; other fenced blocks retain ordinary Markdown code rendering."
Id: "fbcd6e1b-51a9-4fb9-91de-0535332e8e92"

SubsectionTitle: "Page Content Blocks"
Id: "91d0a80f-d191-4d4b-a19b-14f1b94ca619"

Text: "Top-level page content blocks are supported and render directly on the page
rather than inside cards.

- `Title: \"...\"` renders as the page title.
- `SectionTitle: \"...\"` renders as a section heading.
- `SubsectionTitle: \"...\"` renders as a subsection heading.
- `Text: \"...\"` renders prose directly on the page.
- `Section:` was replaced by `SectionTitle:`.
- `Subsection:` was replaced by `SubsectionTitle:`.
- `Subsubsection:` was removed.
- Text literals may span multiple source lines.
- `Text:` content supports Markdown and LaTeX.
- Page headings use the same blue color family as card section labels."
Id: "d96262f7-185a-4677-b505-5cfc33c7ae64"

SubsectionTitle: "Top-Level IDs"
Id: "453aea13-6a51-4c22-9239-72f8f1340684"

Text: "Every top-level item must contain an `Id:` section.

- `mlg check` generates a missing `Id:` before checking the item.
- Generated IDs use a real UUID v4 implementation.
- The generated form is a normal random UUID, not a fixed or mostly-zero value.
- It is an error for two top-level items to have the same ID.
- `mlg check` adds only the missing `Id:` section. It does not add a separator
  line before the ID.
- The view shows the item ID in the hidden card details area.
- Internal item identity should use the `Id:` value rather than deriving identity
  from source position or rendered text."
Id: "59f9fedf-5489-43ec-b0ad-7c279a7beaf9"

SubsectionTitle: "Table Of Contents Files"
Id: "90bee976-e616-4921-9872-286b996857cf"

Text: "Directories may contain a `toc` file that controls the order and visibility of
entries in the left outline.

- If a directory has no `toc`, files are listed alphabetically.
- Fallback display names preserve capitalization semantics already used by the
  view and replace underscores with spaces.
- If a directory has a `toc`, every `.mlg` file in that directory must be listed.
- If a directory has a `toc`, every subdirectory in that directory must be listed.
- A listed file or directory that does not exist is an error.
- A `.mlg` file or directory omitted from the `toc` is an error.
- Each listed entry may be written as just the path, for example:
  `some_file.mlg`.
- Each listed entry may also use `-> HIDDEN` to hide it from the rendered view.
- Each listed entry may use `-> Some Title` to provide a custom display title.
- The same `toc` rules apply independently in each subdirectory."
Id: "9273b584-6595-4fc1-9c19-60d87b3a09bc"

SectionTitle: "Documentation Text And Rendering Names"
Id: "b647d90a-cfe3-48e4-a3c4-b35721fcfe30"

SubsectionTitle: "`called:` And `written:`"
Id: "3780e073-5fd0-4df5-9561-28ff6d53373c"

Text: "`called:` and `written:` have distinct meanings.

- `called:` is non-math text.
- `written:` is math-mode text.
- For `Defines:`, `Declares:`, and `States:`, at least one of `called:` or
  `written:` is required.
- If both are provided, the renderer uses the appropriate one for the context.
- If only `called:` is provided, the missing `written:` text is generated from it.
- If only `written:` is provided, the missing `called:` text is generated from it
  by using the written text in math mode.
- The `called:` text is used for prose labels and card titles.
- The `written:` text is used when the item appears as an expression.
- In rendered `is` statements, the right-hand side uses `written:` when it is
  available and falls back to `called:`.
- Card titles remove placeholder markers such as `?` from rendered title text.
- Generated titles preserve normal lowercase words such as `is`; for example,
  `A? is a subset of B?` renders as `A is a subset of B`, not
  `A Is a subset of B`.
- When an item supplies both, its card title shows the called form, a wide
  space, then the written notation rendered muted. The name always comes first
  and the notation second, whichever order the two are listed in.
- Listing order still decides which single form names the item *inline*, so
  `called:` first makes `X is \empty.set` read \"X is empty set\" while
  `written:` first makes it read \"X is $\emptyset$\".
- A `written:` nested inside a `called:` group documents that called form, so it
  pairs with it in the title just as a top-level `written:` would."
Id: "049b5a49-f1c9-40d8-bb19-02c2b5ad816a"

SubsectionTitle: "`Refines:` Documentation"
Id: "85682aaa-fed1-4409-945f-257d95565b16"

Text: "`Refines:` uses adjective-based documentation.

- `Refines:` entries require an `adjective:` item in `Documented:`.
- `Refines:` entries may contain `written:`.
- `Refines:` entries may not use `called:`.
- When a refined type is rendered, adjectives are printed before the base
  described type.
- Multiple refinements render as comma-separated adjectives, for example
  `injective, surjective function`."
Id: "de287a7e-30fa-483b-b25e-318b0eccb46a"

SubsectionTitle: "Top-Level `Writing:` Blocks"
Id: "8bbedfb4-d1d9-4dc3-a3f4-f821e0abfe76"

Text: "A collection may contain at most one top-level `Writing:` item.

```text
Writing:
. \"alpha :~> \alpha\"
. \"beta :~> \beta\"
```

Rules:

- Each argument is a double-quoted string of the form `\"<name> :~> <latex>\"`.
- The quotes are required because the body after `:~>` is raw non-empty LaTeX
  rather than parsed formulation syntax.
- The left-hand side must be a plain name.
- Every top-level non-prose item may carry its own optional `Writing:` section
  immediately after `Aliases:`, overriding name rendering for that item only.
- The renderer uses these aliases when rendering names in expressions,
  statements, and specifications.
- Names are split on underscores before applying aliases.
- Numeric suffix rendering also honors writing aliases, so `alpha123` renders as
  `\alpha_{123}` when `alpha :~> \alpha` is defined."
Id: "7de0ced3-d722-4660-8eea-2690392cb112"

SubsectionTitle: "Documented Descriptions"
Id: "0e977c40-5523-4973-8e86-73ef46c3c2dc"

Text: "`Documented:` may contain `description:`.

- The text is quoted and may span multiple source lines.
- Common indentation is removed for rendering.
- `mlg view` renders the description as prose above the card.
- Inside the card details, the description item is shown as
  `description: <see above>`."
Id: "a827ee0d-0eb0-4b1b-b859-3dd24baf3e35"

SubsectionTitle: "Command Context Suffixes"
Id: "37744803-7e1d-4507-ba78-8e6bd13ed419"

Text: "Command references may end with a context suffix.

```text
\ordered.pair#using{A := X; B := Y}
\some.theorem#given{X := A; Y := B}
```

Rules:

- `#using{...}` binds values for symbols declared in the referenced command's
  `using:` section.
- `#given{...}` binds values for symbols declared in a referenced theorem-like
  item's `given:` section.
- The suffix appears after all ordinary command curly and parenthesized
  arguments.
- Arguments are separated by semicolons so that individual arguments may contain
  commas.
- `name := value` binds a specific context symbol.
- If there is exactly one context symbol, `#using{value}` or `#given{value}` may
  be used as shorthand.
- The name on the left of `:=` must be a context symbol from the referenced item;
  the value on the right is resolved in the current scope."
Id: "6a3ab485-8190-4ce8-8e20-0af98c6e121d"

SubsectionTitle: "Conditional Documentation Fragments"
Id: "f40e3c8c-d9c4-40a6-b525-51765c1275e6"

Text: "`called:` and `written:` text support conditional fragments.

```text
@[<vars>]{<text-if-present>}:{<text-if-missing>}
```

Rules:

- The `:{<text-if-missing>}` part is optional.
- `<vars>` is a comma-separated list of variable names.
- `@[U]{_{U?}}` outputs its body only if `U` is specified.
- `@[U]{_{U?}}:{_X}` outputs `_X` if `U` is not specified.
- `@[x, y]{...}` requires both `x` and `y` to be specified.
- Conditional fragments may nest.
- Nested fragments are evaluated in the same variable environment as the outer
  text.

Example:

```text
@[x]{x + @[y]{y}:{*}}
```

If `x` and `y` are specified, this renders `x + y`. If `x` is specified and `y`
is not, it renders `x + *`."
Id: "46187699-ff22-4283-a211-e213ab1cbf46"

SectionTitle: "Formulation Syntax"
Id: "c85b9451-55a4-4750-8a3e-340f9950e757"

SubsectionTitle: "Transparent Grouping"
Id: "8437c9cd-fe0f-435f-a44d-7d238d775c27"

Text: "Grouped expressions of the form:

```text
(. x + y .)
```

are supported as source-only grouping.

- The grouping disambiguates the source.
- The grouping parentheses are not rendered.
- `(. x + y .)` renders as `x + y`."
Id: "3e3a4593-1920-4299-9cf8-021cd65d8ca5"

SubsectionTitle: "Optional Command Tails"
Id: "52fdfed8-138e-47ca-a1f5-22dedcbed3a4"

Text: "Optional command tails of the form `:?name{value}` are supported in command
headers and expressions.

- `:?within{U}` declares an optional tail.
- Optional tail variables are allowed in `when:` requirements.
- Optional tail variables are not required in `when:` requirements.
- In expressions, an optional tail is applied only when its value is defined.
- Rendering may use conditional documentation fragments to include or omit text
  based on optional tails."
Id: "fae35f2f-c6f0-4125-8190-10bb18d39388"

SubsectionTitle: "Names With Numeric Suffixes"
Id: "b3bd44c9-e456-4d76-ab90-d234a965aa3b"

Text: "Identifier-like names ending with digits render the digits as subscripts.

- `x1` renders as `x_1`.
- `abc123` renders as `abc_123`.
- This is a rendering rule for names, not a source-level rewrite."
Id: "3002863e-d8f3-4c05-bab3-b39cbb686b3f"

SubsectionTitle: "Builtin Kinds"
Id: "4cc8daa6-8570-4ee6-b995-532c0a8ce88e"

Text: "Builtin kinds render as plain text.

- `\\statement` renders as `statement`.
- `\\expression` renders as `expression`.
- `\\specification` renders as `specification`.
- `\\opaque` is satisfied by any value but does not establish any more specific
  type information.
- `\\abstract` is the abstract target of a `:->` capability.
- `\\type` holds for command references whose top-level entry is a `Defines:`
  item.
- The renderer no longer treats these as a newline plus italic text."
Id: "ac8c3af4-54d4-4c0f-836e-c87caef3f9a1"

SubsectionTitle: "Refined Command Syntax"
Id: "022d692f-3957-4e3b-bedd-a58a0115fd28"

Text: "Refined command headers use the form:

```text
[\(adjective)::base.command:?tail{X}]
```

Rules:

- Only `Refines:` entries may use a refined command header.
- The `Refines:` section contains only the refined subject form.
- The `Refines:` section no longer repeats `is <base>`.
- The base command after `::` comes from the header.
- A refined expression may include multiple refinements, such as
  `\(injective, surjective)::function`.
- A refinement may extend other refinements with:
  `extends: f is \(injective, surjective)::[[f]]`.
- `[[f]]` is valid only in a refined expression of the form
  `\(... )::[[f]]`.
- `[[f]]` means \"the current type of `f`\", allowing the extension to apply to
  a more specific base type such as `bounded.function`."
Id: "fdbf2219-49b5-4c38-8e20-0352a8d3af73"

SubsectionTitle: "Function And Collection Shapes"
Id: "547e3023-7283-45b7-b2ad-60a50217dc5e"

Text: "`Defines:` and `Declares:` support richer target shapes.

Function targets:

- `Defines: f(x_) ::= y_` describes a function-like target with one input.
- `Defines: f(x__) ::= y_` describes a function-like target that accepts any
  number of inputs, treated as a single tuple.
- `Defines: f(x_, y_, z_) ::= w_` describes a function-like target that
  accepts exactly three separate arguments.
- `Defines: f(x_[i_ := 1...n])` declares a ranged variadic mapping input; `x__`
  in that position treats the inputs as a tuple. It must be the only parameter
  and must start at 0 or 1.
- `X ::= x(i_) ::= y_` names the mapping `x`, its alias `X`, its input `i_`, and
  its output `y_`.
- `declares:` on such a `Defines:` target states the input and output
  requirements; component and whole-function declarations are equivalent, so
  `declares: f is (? is \real) -> (? is \real)` is the same as typing `x_` and
  `y_` separately.
- `Declares: h(x__) := f(g(x__)) is \function:on{A}:to{C}` is accepted.

Collection targets:

- `Defines: X ::= {x__ : ...}` describes a collection shape whose elements
  may have any arity and are treated as a tuple.
- `Defines: X ::= {x_ : ...}` describes a collection shape accepting a single
  value, where that value may itself have any expression shape.
- `member_of` is a keyword used by enabled membership capabilities.
- `x member_of X` is valid only when `X` is a collection literal or has a
  collection literal attached by a build.
- `A := \set@{x_ : x_ is \real}` builds a collection literal at the described
  type and records the literal for `A`.
- `A := {x_ : x_ is \real | x_ > 2} is \set` binds the literal to `A` as a set.
- If `A := \set@{x_ : x_ is \real}` and `x \"in\" A`, the checker can establish
  `x is \real` through a `from:` capability.
- If `A is \set` without a collection literal, membership establishes
  `x is \\opaque`.

Literal arguments also satisfy a required nominal type structurally: a function
literal by its input arity and input/output specs, a tuple literal by component
count and positional specs, and a collection literal against the described
element pattern and element specs."
Id: "8fad122e-d0e5-4970-8cbb-88aa13765516"

SubsectionTitle: "Set Builder Definitions"
Id: "657dfbab-536e-482f-a0ba-81cc6cc5b735"

Text: "Set builder definitions allow general element forms before the colon.

- A set builder may use a name, tuple, function form, or other valid form in
  the binder position.
- For example, `{(a_, b_) : a_ \"in\" A, b_ \"in\" B}` is accepted.
- This applies in declarations and definitions such as:
  `Defines: C := {(a_, b_) : ...} is \set`."
Id: "ee6486f7-157f-4564-9caa-5dbf4897847e"

SubsectionTitle: "Function Literals And Function Types"
Id: "9e14de40-5796-427c-a992-53247e6d242e"

Text: "A function literal is an anonymous function value written with `=>`, while a
function *type* uses `->`. Keeping the two arrows distinct mirrors the `:=>` and
`:->` operator forms.

- `(x_ is \real) => x_ + 1` binds the parameter through the parenthesized spec
  so the body checks.
- `(x_ \"in\" A) => x_ + a` is also valid; the parenthesized left side types the
  parameter.
- A bare `x_ => ...` (no spec) is only valid where the parameter's type can be
  inferred from a declared function type, as in `f := x_ => x_ + 1 is
  \real.function`.
- The command sugar `\foo[x_ is \real]{x_ + 1}` expands to
  `\foo{(x_ is \real) => x_ + 1}`.
- `->` accepts one or more input specs and exactly one output spec, so unary,
  binary, and n-ary function types are written directly:
  `f is (_ \"in\" A, _ \"in\" B) -> (_ \"in\" C)`. The declared input arity is
  retained rather than curried into nested unary types.
- Function-type spec parameters must be written `_`, or `?` in a structural
  type literal such as `(? is \natural) -> (? is \real)`.
- A mapping body is checked after resolving type-directed operators, so an
  overloaded expression such as `x + y` contributes the output type of the
  selected addition operator. When that operator is declared through a callable
  type, the checker follows the type's `extends:` chain to its function codomain
  and uses the codomain as the application's output spec."
Id: "c4744318-52a4-49d5-b41d-c0d9e08cf12d"

SubsectionTitle: "Spec Literals And `satisfies`"
Id: "6da24db4-1501-4c7f-bfc8-29ff63db6592"

Text: "A spec literal is a reusable value of the builtin type `\\specification` with an
implicit `?` placeholder subject.

- `? is \real` and `? \"in\" \reals` are spec literals. The `\"op\"` target may be a
  name or a command such as `\reals`.
- The infix operator `satisfies` applies a spec to a subject: `x satisfies (? is
  \real)` substitutes `?` with `x`, yielding `x is \real`.
- This enables general set builders, for example
  `\set:where{? is \real}` whose membership reduces to `x is \real`."
Id: "b511e0f8-b2a0-4628-98ae-7b05fd04ae09"

SubsectionTitle: "Inferred Parameters"
Id: "b0e40152-a152-45bd-8eaf-9160c8e767f7"

Text: "In a declaring position a command argument may be written `X?` to declare `X`
inline with the exact type that argument position requires.

- `f is \function:from{A?}:to{B?}` introduces `A` and `B` as `\set` (from the
  command's `when:`), so a separate `A, B is \set` is unnecessary.
- The first occurrence carries the `?`; later uses are the plain name.
- Reusing `X?` when `X` is already in scope is an error.
- The `?` is authoring-only and renders as `X`."
Id: "d37421ed-107b-4537-b053-1fa50f1583a8"

SubsectionTitle: "Specifications Versus Predicates (`is` versus `is?`)"
Id: "38817908-3c89-4477-85ca-39a49894ac0c"

Text: "A bare specification introduces symbols, so it is accepted only in binding
positions, not where a statement is expected.

- A specification is `x is \type`, an infix spec command `A \:subset:/ B`, or a
  named spec `x \"op\" y`.
- Specifications are allowed in binding positions: `exists:`, `given:`,
  `forAll:` arguments, and `where:` / `such_that:` / `when:`.
- In statement positions — `if:`, `then:`, `iff:`, `that:`, `else:`, and inside
  `not:` / `allOf:` / `anyOf:` / `oneOf:` / `equivalently:` — use the predicate
  forms instead: `is?`, `is_not?`, and the `?`-suffixed infix spec `A \:subset?:/
  B`.
- A named spec statement such as `x \"in\" y` is a statement (not a symbol
  introducer) and is accepted in statement positions as written."
Id: "e527cba5-a73e-41ee-a84a-432ef654060e"

SubsectionTitle: "Variadic Command Arguments"
Id: "2a8821a7-2b32-4790-8285-68b1ceea11f1"

Text: "Only curly argument groups can be variadic; a variadic parameter occupies the
whole `{...}` group.

- The forms are `x...`, `x...n`, and `x[i_ := 0...]` / `x[i_ := 1...]` /
  `x[i_ := 0...n]` / `x[i_ := 1...n]`. No other starting index is accepted.
- Reusing a length name constrains groups to the same number of arguments, so
  `[\foo{x...n}:bar{y...n}]` requires equal lengths while
  `[\foo{x...n}:bar{y...m}]` leaves them independent.
- Slices `x...`, `x[0...n]`, `x[1...n]`, `x[0...i_...n]`, and `x[1...i_...n]`
  broadcast over `:=`, `=`, `!=`, `is`, `is?`, and quoted specification
  operators. Paired slices must spell exactly the same start, binder, and end.
- In a clause list a broadcast produces one clause per element.
- `\\map{...}:to{...}` zips aligned slices pointwise; `\\leftReduce{op}:on{...}`
  and `\\rightReduce{op}:on{...}` reduce one slice and render with an ellipsis.
- `x...` renders as `x_1, ..., x_.`, and `x[1...i_...n]` as
  `x_1, ..., x_i, ..., x_n`.

A curly group may instead declare a rectangular two-dimensional parameter such
as `x[(i_, j_) := (1,1)...(m,n)]`.

- Invocations separate columns with commas and rows with semicolons.
- Row and column counts bind independently, and elements are referenced as
  `x[i,j]`.
- Ragged matrices, empty dimensions, flat arguments, and 2D syntax in a
  non-curly group are rejected.
- Two-axis selections give each axis as an index, a range, or `...`, and
  broadcast under the same relations as 1D slices.
- Written templates nest element notation inside row notation, so
  `\left[ x?{{...,...}\\} \right]` renders the argument as a matrix. Symbolic
  definition-card titles keep the same 2D row shape."
Id: "e23ab977-d2f3-4201-91d5-1569ac13e3d1"

SubsectionTitle: "Mapping Parameter Selectors"
Id: "d6a975a3-dbfb-46ed-8d48-e15fb290d8ab"

Text: "An ordinary command heading may select parameters from a mapping declared in
another curly group.

- An exact selector uses the mapping's declared name (`f.x_`); an arbitrary
  selector introduces a fresh name (`f.u?_`); a variadic selector names a subset
  of a ranged variadic mapping (`f.x_[i_[j_:=1...m]]`).
- Selectors must occupy exactly one curly group, share one owner, and that owner
  must be declared with explicit parameters in exactly one other curly group.
- An arbitrary selector name must not duplicate a declared parameter name.
- Selectors are supported only by ordinary headings, not by infix, infix-spec,
  or refined headings.
- At a use site the mapping parameters are supplied by command mapping-literal
  sugar such as `\integral[x_, y_ is \real]{x_^2 + y_^2}:d{x_}`, and every
  selected value must be one of those bound parameters.
- Such a heading gets a specialized signature (`\integral{_(2)}:d{#1}`) in
  addition to its general signature (`\integral:d`). Specialized signatures must
  be unique; several definitions may share a general signature.
- Resolution prefers a fixed mapping arity over `_(*)`, then exact positions in
  written order over `#?`, and finally `#*`. Equally specific candidates are an
  ambiguity error."
Id: "e6d1b49d-4aa4-4dbd-b504-874ef59c8943"

SubsectionTitle: "Labeled Formulations And `Justification:`"
Id: "d99f3aed-bf7f-44a0-a633-c1fc3d0cc5ca"

Text: "A grouped expression, statement, or specification may carry a `[:label:]`, and a
top-level `Justification:` section discharges those labels.

- `Justification:` appears immediately after `Documented:` and holds
  `have:`/`asserting:` groups, each with a required `[label]` heading.
- A matching entry establishes the labeled formulation using its `asserting:`
  items, exactly as an inline `have:`/`asserting:` group would.
- An entry's `have:` must restate the labeled formulation.
- Every entry must be referenced by some labeled formulation; an unmatched label
  is checked inline as usual.
- Labels are allowed on nested formulations, including inside a `declares:` item
  or a `satisfies:` clause.
- The optional `because:` and `by:` sections are justification the checker only
  reference-validates; it never proves them.
- `Justification:` replaces the older `Justified:` section and its `label:` /
  `by:` prose items."
Id: "b38e23ec-3695-437a-9158-1af03b52da7b"

SubsectionTitle: "New And Renamed Top-Level Items"
Id: "d432d4fe-d9f5-4829-a269-adc506f3222d"

Text: "The top-level inventory changed in several places.

- `Describes:` was renamed to `Defines:`, and the older `Defines:` was renamed
  to `Declares:`. The `specifies:` section of the old `Describes:` is now
  `declares:`.
- `Corollary:` was removed; a result that follows from another is written as an
  ordinary `Theorem:`.
- `Conjecture:` was added. It shares the `Axiom:`/`Theorem:` shape and records a
  statement that is not claimed to have a proof.
- `TextTheorem:`, `TextAxiom:`, `TextConjecture:`, and `TextDefinition:` were
  added as opaque prose placeholders. Their bodies are never parsed as
  MathLingua, their `Documented:` accepts only `called:`, `written:`,
  `description:`, and `notes:`, and `Id:` is required.
- `Refines:` gained the optional, mutually exclusive `implicitly:` and
  `explicitly:` marker sections immediately after `Refines:`. Both require the
  refined base type to be a subtype of another type; `implicitly:` requires the
  body to contain only the inherited `extends:` clause naming the parent type's
  refinement, and `explicitly:` requires at least one property beyond it.
- The `let:` clause group was added: it introduces local bindings, assumes an
  optional `where:` guard, and checks `then:` with those available."
Id: "78caeb72-ce47-4cd8-a051-4a97f35928c6"

SubsectionTitle: "Placeholder Parentheses"
Id: "d8fd757f-129c-4b76-91ed-1178af1d515d"

Text: "A placeholder in a `called:` or `written:` template may carry a `+` or `-` before
its `?` to control the parentheses around the substituted value. The modifier is
not part of the name, so `A?`, `A+?`, and `A-?` all substitute the same value.

- `A?` substitutes the value exactly as rendered.
- `A+?` wraps the value in exactly one pair of parentheses, unless it is a
  single atom. It never doubles parentheses: the value is first reduced to its
  bare form and then wrapped once.
- `A-?` removes every pair of parentheses wrapping the value. Only parentheses
  enclosing the *whole* value are removed, so `(1+2)+(3+4)` is left intact.

A value counts as a single atom when it contains no space or comma outside a
bracket — names, single commands, and function calls do, while compound
expressions and comma-separated tuples do not.

A modifier only applies when the `+` or `-` sits between the name and the `?`,
so `A?-B?` is still a placeholder, a literal `-`, and another placeholder. Where
a template is rendered without values, a modifier has nothing to act on and
shows the same bare name that `A?` does."
Id: "e2db2f93-7aeb-4f97-871b-5b40ccf21a4a"

SectionTitle: "Semantic Checks"
Id: "849f1ed5-23d0-4ec8-9b17-95c08205d22a"

SubsectionTitle: "Editor Language Server"
Id: "83a216ba-176c-41aa-9c27-0fbd4f22932a"

Text: "`mlg lsp` provides diagnostics on open and save; context-aware completion for
group heads, section items, and commands; jump to definition; and workspace
rename for top-level command signatures and uses."
Id: "916bf3ea-6510-4762-b896-8833e97bf227"

SubsectionTitle: "`when:` Requirements"
Id: "c5fffa4d-ed78-4b3e-b1b4-77bb2bf87cc7"

Text: "Definition-like entries validate `when:` against the parameters introduced by
their headers.

Rules:

- Required non-optional header parameters must have a corresponding `when:`
  requirement.
- Optional tail parameters are allowed in `when:` but are not required unless a
  `Defines:` entry references them in semantic constraints such as
  `declares:`, `extends:`, or `satisfies:`.
- Target symbols introduced by a declaration target such as `G ::= (X, *, e)`
  are not `when:` parameters unless they also occur in the command header.
- Target symbols introduced by `Defines:`, `Declares:`, and `Refines:` targets
  must have specifications directly, such as through `declares:`, `using:`, or
  an `is` relation, or transitively through `extends: ... via ...`.
- A `Refines:` target symbol may also be inherited: a symbol the refined base
  type already declares counts as specified.
- A mapping-parameter selector is bound by its associated mapping form, so it is
  allowed but not required as a separate `when:` subject.
- For a two-dimensional variadic parameter, `x is T` constrains the value as a
  whole while `x[..., ...] is T` is instantiated once per supplied cell.
- `A, B is \set` counts as both `A is \set` and `B is \set`.
- `P, Q is \\statement` counts as both `P is \\statement` and
  `Q is \\statement`.
- `when:` clauses only support:
  - `<subject> is <type>`
  - `<subject> \"op\" <target>`
- Assignments, definitions, and arbitrary expressions are not valid `when:`
  clauses."
Id: "638d76f1-1792-4e44-ab03-1722e1ae91c6"

SubsectionTitle: "Symbol Scoping"
Id: "12f24699-3d43-418f-a227-7a5c7b599662"

Text: "The checker reports any ordinary symbol use that has not been introduced.

- Binding and assumption sections such as `given:`, `exists:`, `existsUnique:`,
  and `forAll:` introduce their declared subjects.
- Clause-group `given:`, `exists:`, `existsUnique:`, and `forAll:` sections may
  contain multiple block arguments; each argument is introduced in order before
  the guard, predicate, or body is checked.
- Declaration definitions make declaration-side symbols available to the right
  hand side, so `f(x_) := x_` is valid.
- Declaration relations are checked too, so `Defines: f(x_) := x_ is
  \function:on{A}:to{B}` requires `B` to have been introduced.
- Membership assumptions bind the member side, but the collection side must
  already be declared.
- Explicit optional command tail arguments are checked for undeclared symbols
  even when the tail is inactive for requirement matching."
Id: "eaf616f8-53c0-4457-8819-19026f293f6b"

SubsectionTitle: "Existential Clauses"
Id: "72810ff0-9484-4519-91af-aa0a99bd43c4"

Text: "Existential clause groups support optional predicates. Bindings introduced by a
quantifier or by `let:` are local to the clause group that introduces them.

- `exists: x is \real` is valid without a `suchThat:` section.
- `existsUnique: x is \real` is valid without a `suchThat:` section.
- If `suchThat:` is present, it must contain one or more clauses.
- A present `suchThat:` section is checked the same way as before.
- If `suchThat:` is omitted, the clause still introduces the existential
  binding inside the existential's child context, but has no predicate clauses
  to assume."
Id: "32a8c3c7-4d3b-4c6c-9e82-079813fd2b9d"

SubsectionTitle: "Type Facts And Extensions"
Id: "af07b009-13f2-41a8-a358-01ae6fa9c8e8"

Text: "The checker uses simple type facts and extension facts.

- `extends: X is \set` lets an item described by the refined type be used where
  a set is required.
- `extends: G is \set via X` records the extension through the given structural
  component.
- Facts introduced by `given:`, `when:`, `extends:`, `declares:`, and enabled
  membership capabilities are available while checking dependent statements.
- The `via` form sets the types of the symbols it names, so `extends: M is \set
  via X` records `X is \set` and a `via` tuple maps positionally onto the
  extended type's own components.
- Refined command type expressions are reference-checked, but a fact such as
  `f is \(continuous)::function:on{A}:to{B}` does not currently become a usable
  type fact for proving later requirements.
- This is type establishment, not theorem proving."
Id: "b785d9b8-e3b1-412c-a60a-c84f12bbf853"

SubsectionTitle: "`Defines:` And `Declares:` Usage"
Id: "2a45224c-2be3-47e3-8311-6b6d1aef88ac"

Text: "The checker distinguishes values, definitions, and described types.

- `X is \foo` is used when `\foo` is a `Defines:` entry.
- `X := \foo` is used when `\foo` is a `Declares:` entry.
- `X := \set@{...}` is valid where a definition-style binding is expected, and
  is the way a `Declares:` value states its type without `is`.
- A `Declares:` entry may include an expression and result type, such as
  `Declares: C := A is \set`."
Id: "2437c74f-d2f9-461c-8504-5e9db80bdccf"

SectionTitle: "Operators, Symbols, And Disambiguation"
Id: "55f87800-682a-428b-a258-8eac57a62134"

SubsectionTitle: "Command Headers For Operators And Functions"
Id: "a8d45e32-3a99-44ae-9f68-1c01773e86fe"

Text: "Command headers may define special operators, named operators, and functions.

Examples:

```text
[x_ + y_]
[x_ |op| y_]
[f(x_)]
[f| x_]
[x_ |f]
```

Rules:

- These headers do not use colon-directed forms such as `:|op|`.
- Special operators are not limited to `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `=`, or `^`.
- Any sequence of special operator characters may be an operator.
- Operators may include named suffixes such as `*_1` or `*_free`, and may carry
  trailing primes, as in `*'`, `*''`, and `*'_a`.
- A capability may declare a bracketed placeholder operator `x_ [*] y_`, whose
  symbol is drawn from the definition's inputs or `Defines:` target rather than
  being a fixed character.
- Prefix and postfix named operators are treated as one-argument functions.
- Infix named operators are treated as two-argument functions.
- `f| x` is equivalent to `f(x)`.
- `x |f` is equivalent to `f(x)`.
- `x |op| y` is equivalent to `op(x, y)`.
- `x * y` is equivalent to `` `*`(x, y) ``."
Id: "9dac0024-8168-45c3-9472-5dc107c3e678"

SubsectionTitle: "Type-Directed Operator Resolution"
Id: "3490fe5f-8046-4553-a9a1-9cf44089806e"

Text: "Type-directed operator forms are supported.

- `x :- y` resolves `-` from the type of `x`.
- `x -: y` resolves `-` from the type of `y`.
- `x :-: y` resolves `-` from the least common ancestor type of `x` and `y`.
- If both operands have the same type, `x :-: y` resolves from that type.
- The same rule applies to named operators, for example `x :|op| y`,
  `x |op|: y`, and `x :|op|: y`.
- Resolution searches the `Requires:` and `Enables:` capabilities on the
  selected type and its parent types.
- It is an error if the operator is not enabled by the selected type hierarchy.
- It is an error if the resolved operator's requirements do not match the
  operands."
Id: "c8291ec8-5c0e-4a47-bf6a-a6c137552c0e"

SubsectionTitle: "Plain Operator Resolution"
Id: "8430f827-6042-43bc-87c0-9fa294aec321"

Text: "Plain operators such as `x - y` use scope and disambiguation.

- The checker first searches local scope, moving outward.
- If no local definition is found, it searches global command definitions.
- If a matching `Disambiguates:` entry exists, branches are considered in order.
- The first `when:` branch whose requirements match the operands is used.
- If no branch matches and an `else:` branch exists, the `else:` branch is used.
- A `Disambiguates:` entry may contain only an `else:` branch.
- If no definition or applicable disambiguation is found, the operator is an
  error.
- Plain `=` and `!=` are exceptions: they may be written for any operand types
  without a definition. If the common operand type enables `=` or `!=`, that
  capability is still used.
- Fallback `=` and `!=` expressions are treated as statements, so they can be
  passed to commands that require `\\statement`."
Id: "857a84d1-0c41-4ae1-957d-fe6347285328"

SubsectionTitle: "`Requires:` And `Enables:`"
Id: "7cea31c3-656d-411c-87ad-0bee07c183cd"

Text: "Types can now separate definitional requirements from additional capabilities.

- `Requires:` is accepted on command-backed top-level entries that support
  `Enables:`.
- `Requires:` must appear before `Enables:` when both are present.
- `Requires:` accepts `capability:` groups.
- `Requires:` accepts `definition:` groups of the form `\command is <spec>`.
- Capabilities from `Requires:` and `Enables:` are unioned for type checking.
- `Requires:` is intended for operations that are part of the definition of a
  construct.
- `Enables:` is intended for additional supported operations that come from
  other definitions.
- A `Requires.definition:` item succeeds only when the referenced command is a
  top-level `Declares:` item and that definition's output facts establish the
  requested `is <spec>` fact.
- A `Requires.definition:` item fails if the referenced command is undefined,
  is not a `Declares:` entry, or does not establish the requested fact.
- `Enables:` accepts cast-backed `from:` groups.
- A `from:` group must contain exactly one of `capability:` or `as:`.
- `from: ... capability:` capabilities are used only when the actual target has
  a recorded cast or set literal.
- When reducing a `from: ... capability:` rule, the source subject from the
  `from:` declaration is substituted with the actual target value.
- Ordinary non-`from:` capabilities on opaque targets no longer read cast
  literals through `member_of`; literal-backed membership requires a structural
  target or an explicit `from:` capability.
- `from: ... as:` records and validates an expression binding that describes
  how to view the cast source as the described form.
- A `from: ... as:` binding can reduce facts about a casted function call by
  matching the binding's left side against the call and substituting the right
  side into facts from the cast literal.
- `Enables:` accepts `relation:` groups with required `to:` declarations and
  optional `when:`, `means:`, `represents:`, and `by:` sections.
- The `:= ...` construction in a `relation:` `to:` declaration is optional.
- A `relation:`'s cast kind is set with `represents:`, whose value is
  `\\coercion` or `\\encoding` (this replaced the older `as: \\view` /
  `as: \\abstraction`).
- `relation:` entries marked `represents: \\coercion` provide relationships
  reachable with a soft build `\type@value` (or `x is \type`).
- `relation:` entries marked `represents: \\encoding` provide an abstraction
  boundary crossed only by a hard build `\type@!value` (or `x is! \type`).
- The removed `value as \type` / `value as! \type` cast syntax was replaced by
  these builds.
- An unmarked `relation:` is still valid; it records the relationship for
  readers without affecting builds.
- Coercion relationships may satisfy requirements after a command or operator
  has already resolved, but are not used to resolve operators or capabilities."
Id: "6db6781e-cfb3-4c18-b31d-36686c7f831f"

SubsectionTitle: "Capability Rules"
Id: "ada03fc6-67c0-48d3-8adf-29e6c2fb08b0"

Text: "`Requires:` and `Enables:` capabilities define type-specific notation.

Rules for `:=>`:

- In an infix capability such as `x_ - y_ :=> ...`, both operands are treated as
  values of the type currently being described.
- In prefix or postfix capabilities, the single operand is treated as a value
  of the type currently being described.
- The right-hand side may use the described subject and the capability operands.

Rules for `:->`:

- The right-hand side must be the item being defined by the capability.
- The left-hand side does not receive an implicit type from the described item.
- This is used for specification operators such as membership.

Function and value capabilities:

- A function capability such as `f(x_) :=> \foo{X, x_}` is used as `X.f(a)`.
- Arguments of such function capabilities do not receive an implicit type.
- A bare capability such as `a :=> \some.value{X}` is used as `X.a`.
- A callable-owner capability may use the described subject as the function
  name, for example `R(a_, b_) :-> (a_, b_) \"in\" R`.
- If `R is \relation:from{A}:to{B}`, then `R(a, b)` resolves through that
  capability, reducing to the capability target with `R`, `a_`, and `b_`
  substituted.
- A callable-owner capability's `written:` text is used by `mlg view` to render
  matching calls, for example `written: \"a_? \: R \: b_?\"` renders `R(a, b)` as
  `a \: R \: b`.
- All capabilities have access to the subject of the item being
  described.

Set expression literals now also accept the unconstrained ellipsis form, such as
`{(p_, q_) : ...}`. This is useful in `from:` declarations that describe the
shape of an accepted cast source without adding element constraints."
Id: "1fc4e3d3-6624-4d61-99f9-43ca75520433"

SubsectionTitle: "Built-In `\\type`"
Id: "9cab9fc7-6963-45e3-9792-9371a625d91d"

Text: "The checker supports the built-in type predicate `\\type`.

- `\foo is? \\type` succeeds when `\foo` is a top-level `Defines:` entry.
- `\foo is? \\type` fails when `\foo` is a `Declares:` entry.
- `\foo is_not? \\type` succeeds when `\foo` is not a described type.
- Ordinary built-in type facts share the same fact-checking path as
  `\\statement`, `\\expression`, and `\\specification`."
Id: "711bef31-9baf-4274-9c30-c342f2b73326"

SectionTitle: "Rendering And View"
Id: "9f5e8442-997a-48b3-8be5-75fe866886b5"

SubsectionTitle: "Recent Viewer Behavior"
Id: "697b0133-6559-4130-a410-58b01ff76d4c"

Text: "- The viewer listens on `0.0.0.0` while reporting a localhost URL.
- Routes are warmed in the background and display loading skeletons.
- Directory display names come from the directory name unless a `toc` override
  supplies another title."
Id: "02164525-c686-45fc-84de-17c406dc01d3"

SubsectionTitle: "Math Rendering"
Id: "0a2a4cc9-c9bb-48e8-b64c-9e5b2f7141af"

Text: "Rendering behavior was tightened in several places.

- `(. ... .)` source groups do not render visible parentheses.
- Defined command references render according to their documented `written:`
  or derived written form.
- `called:` content is rendered in text mode.
- `written:` content is rendered in math mode.
- If an axiom, theorem, or similar entry has no explicit name, its label is
  converted to a display name by replacing dots with spaces and capitalizing
  words.
- For example, `\axiom.of.unordered.pair` renders as
  `Axiom Of Unordered Pair`.
- LaTeX package support includes the packages needed for common math such as
  `\emptyset`.
- There is no arbitrary fallback that special-cases commands such as
  `\empty.set`."
Id: "7205d75e-bc65-44a8-bc07-91fbd517f3b4"

SubsectionTitle: "Card View"
Id: "ac43cc0b-4e6e-4461-b1c8-7ca709a48705"

Text: "The card view includes source and detail controls.

- Each top-level card has a subtle source icon in the top-right corner.
- Clicking the icon flips the card to show syntax-colored MathLingua source.
- The source back side has a white background and no gray border, so the source
  appears directly on the card.
- Source view margins and padding are compact.
- `Documented:` is hidden behind the card expander.
- `Enables:` is also hidden behind the card expander.
- The item `Id:` appears in the hidden details area."
Id: "507d6424-bc4f-469f-b4eb-2b59069b0203"

SubsectionTitle: "Definition Drilldown"
Id: "db72dee6-db72-4217-b604-a254bf35115c"

Text: "Clickable definitions in cards open related definition cards below the current
card.

- The definition area is rendered as an inset, etched region.
- The inset region has its own close button that closes all cards in that
  region.
- Individual close buttons remove only the selected definition card.
- Clicking another definition appends that card to the top of the definition
  list.
- Opening a new definition does not replace or remove previously opened
  definition cards.
- Definition cards can themselves contain clickable definitions, allowing a
  vertical chain of exploration."
Id: "c177d7aa-1e8c-408e-ab64-97b9d969708c"

SubsectionTitle: "Navigation And Outline"
Id: "ae6caeec-21cd-4bac-91f2-b0fe0dccee36"

Text: "The viewer has responsive navigation behavior.

- Pages have subtle previous and next navigation buttons.
- The buttons show only the destination section names.
- The first page does not show a previous button.
- The last page does not show a next button.
- On desktop-width clients, the left outline is open by default.
- On narrow screens and mobile, the left outline is closed by default.
- On narrow screens and mobile, selecting an entry closes the outline.
- On desktop, selecting an entry keeps the outline open.
- Initial render state is aligned between server and client to avoid hydration
  mismatches.
- Loading a specific route no longer flashes the first table-of-contents page
  before switching to the requested page.
- Directory names in the outline use the same font sizing as other outline
  entries."
Id: "910e5e50-1de2-44b4-9f95-fe63275c0727"

SubsectionTitle: "Refresh Behavior"
Id: "b524e28b-687c-47bc-a1a7-60dc849df7e7"

Text: "`mlg view` refresh behavior was changed.

- Refreshing the browser reparses the MathLingua source.
- The user does not need to stop and restart `mlg view` to see source changes.
- If the updated source has errors, the view keeps the last valid rendered data.
- In that error case, standard output shows the errors that prevented the view
  from updating."
Id: "48a169d3-b3ba-4402-bbc3-a55433b2de74"

SectionTitle: "CLI"
Id: "ae58ad36-ee2b-49f9-b864-09d32422ca55"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg.json` Configuration"
Id: "de8b85c1-f205-4793-be0b-ec8f841ec851"

Text: "Every collection carries an `mlg.json` at its root that spells out its whole
configuration; `mlg init` writes it with all fields at their defaults, and
`mlg check` reports any that are missing.

- `name` — the collection name (string), used as the `mlg view` title.
- `version` — the collection version (string, default `\"0\"`).
- `margin` — the target line width for `mlg format` (positive integer, default
  `80`). This field was renamed from `print_margin`; a config still carrying the
  old key is reported as an error.
- `formatOnCheck` — whether `mlg check` formats the collection before checking
  it (boolean, default `true`).
- `outputDir` — where `mlg export` writes the static site (a non-empty relative
  path inside the collection, default `\"docs\"`)."
Id: "b1e6b2b8-5753-466d-b99d-3b8d1e2eb7df"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg format`"
Id: "e4052fa1-1a83-47be-b4c1-000ad6ee0a35"

Text: "`mlg format` normalizes the source of the whole collection to the configured
`margin` width without changing its meaning: it fixes the blank-line gap between
top-level items to two lines and reflows quoted `\"...\"` text to the margin. A
LaTeX blob (`$...$`, `$$...$$`, `\(...\)`, `\[...\]`) is kept atomic, and a text
value containing a multi-line or over-wide blob is left unchanged. Editing is
line-based, so nothing but the reflowed text moves, and the result still passes
`mlg check`."
Id: "7a374de1-a055-4724-8bb9-a1b97d8e78aa"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg check` Formatting"
Id: "9eef8771-0458-44e3-a93b-505883ba93f2"

Text: "When `formatOnCheck` is `true` (the default), `mlg check` runs `mlg format`
before checking so that reported source positions match the formatted files. It
also inserts a missing top-level `Id:` (a UUID v4) before checking."
Id: "71c9f8c6-2eac-47bd-adfd-32f40d6047b0"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg clean`"
Id: "8bb86754-90d0-41a9-86c4-74c910120300"

Text: "`mlg clean` removes the generated `docs/` output directory; it is the inverse of
`mlg export` and is a no-op when there is nothing to remove."
Id: "b7cd04b6-db58-40f7-87fb-60e298848f07"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg release`"
Id: "629008e8-724e-4867-9bc5-de74889873d0"

Text: "`mlg release` records a version snapshot of the collection after checking it. It
requires `--summary <text>` and supports `--dry-run` (show what would be
recorded without writing files) and `--diff` (also show a diff of each changed
item)."
Id: "b03dd668-0174-4153-86e1-9c20f8b29e60"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg export`"
Id: "9a5db441-4491-4ba1-850d-c73d77defe56"

Text: "`mlg export` builds a static viewer into the `docs/` directory after checking
the collection. It supports `--force`, `--base-path`, and `--cname`, and writes
the route data, `.nojekyll`, and optional `CNAME` needed for static hosting."
Id: "ddf81812-806b-494f-9a86-e26b62f18a9f"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg debug`"
Id: "5318d64e-82ef-4f00-9d62-9e6783f714da"

Text: "A hidden `mlg debug` command was added for parser exploration.

- It opens a textual user interface.
- The user can select formulation, structural, or command-header mode.
- The left panel is text input.
- The right panel shows parse errors and the parse tree.
- Parse trees are pretty-printed with real newlines and nested structure rather
  than a single escaped string.
- The command is hidden from normal help output where supported by the CLI
  framework."
Id: "355e0b78-e800-4497-8d20-4af8e36e85e0"

SubsectionTitle: "`mlg whte_rbt.obj`"
Id: "d3215fcd-a8a9-47d4-94f9-d9df98514eaf"

Text: "A hidden easter-egg command was added.

- The command is `mlg whte_rbt.obj`.
- It prints the Jurassic Park style security-interface transcript.
- It types commands with small delays.
- It ends by repeatedly printing `YOU DIDN'T SAY THE MAGIC WORD!`.
- The command is hidden from normal help output where supported by the CLI
  framework."
Id: "e50bef52-6be8-4a2f-9ff9-270560d95107"

SectionTitle: "Error Reporting"
Id: "2f14e27c-6397-4a0f-aad6-d6c2b01f7fb6"

Text: "Error messages were made more user-facing.

- Requirement failures now say `Could not establish requirement` rather than
  `Could not prove requirement`.
- The wording avoids implying theorem proving.
- AST debug representations are avoided in user-facing messages.
- Expressions in diagnostics are rendered in source-like form, such as `A - B`.
- Paths under the configured content directory are shortened. For example,
  `content/sets/set.mlg` is reported as `sets/set.mlg`.
- Line and column information is reported where the checker can locate the
  relevant source span.
- Parser errors surfaced through `mlg debug` are formatted for readability."
Id: "97e93247-bae4-420a-9a78-7ea3518214d8"
